            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-6">Selected Publications</h2>
            <div class="space-y-4">

              <div class="card">
                <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-foreground mb-1">*Blower, E., *Oliver, S., (2021). Workplace Experiences and Career Development of Retail Managers: Management Practice, Professional Learning and Organisational Support. <em>University of Northumbria.</em></p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2"><strong>Abstract</strong></p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-1">Retail managers operate at the intersection of organisational strategy, workforce management and customer-facing delivery, frequently within working environments characterised by demanding performance expectations, extended working hours, changing consumer behaviour and continuous operational pressure. Despite the central contribution of retail managers to organisational performance, comparatively limited attention has been given to how their everyday workplace experiences influence their professional development, wellbeing, management capability and longer-term career progression.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">This paper examines the workplace experiences and career development of retail managers through the interconnected perspectives of management practice, management development and human resource management. Building upon Blower's doctoral research into the experiences of managers working within the retail sector, the paper explores the personal and professional challenges associated with managing retail operations and considers how managers develop the knowledge, confidence and capabilities required to progress within increasingly complex organisational environments.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">Particular attention is given to the relationship between workplace learning, formal management education, organisational support and individual career development. The paper considers the extent to which management capability is developed experientially through the realities of managing people, performance, customers and organisational change, while recognising that experience alone may be insufficient to prepare managers for increasingly senior leadership responsibilities. It therefore examines the value of combining workplace experience with structured professional development, mentoring, management education and opportunities for critical reflection.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">The study further considers the role of human resource management practices in creating organisational environments that support both performance and individual development. Issues including workload, wellbeing, career planning, professional recognition, access to training, managerial support and opportunities for progression are considered as important influences on employee engagement and the retention and development of management talent.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">The paper argues for a more integrated approach to retail management development in which professional learning, personal wellbeing and career progression are treated as interconnected rather than separate organisational concerns. It proposes that retail organisations should move beyond episodic management training towards structured developmental pathways combining workplace learning, formal education, mentoring, reflective practice and continuing professional development.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">The paper concludes that investment in the development and wellbeing of retail managers represents not simply a human resource intervention but a strategic organisational capability. Creating supportive environments in which managers can learn, develop and progress has the potential to strengthen management practice, improve employee experience, enhance retention and build the leadership capability required for the future of the retail sector.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-3"><strong>Keywords:</strong> Retail Management; Management Practice; Management Development; Human Resource Management; Workplace Experience; Workplace Learning; Career Development; Employee Wellbeing; Professional Development; Leadership Development</p>
              </div>

              <div class="card">
                <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-foreground mb-1">~Oliver, S., ~Salimi, Z., ~Blower, E., *Wu, X., *Oliver, P., G., (2026). From Supervisory Dependence to Researcher Independence: Exploring the Development of Independent Learning Across the Doctoral Journey. <em>~Brenkley Doctoral College. *Edinburgh Napier University.</em></p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2"><strong>Abstract</strong></p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-1">The development of independence is widely regarded as a defining outcome of doctoral education. However, doctoral researchers do not necessarily enter their programmes as fully independent researchers. Independence develops progressively through experience, supervision, critical reflection, increasing research responsibility and engagement with the wider scholarly community. Understanding how this transition occurs, and whether established theories of learning adequately explain it, has important implications for doctoral supervision, programme design and researcher development.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">This study investigates the development of independent learning across different stages of the doctoral journey through interviews with 40 doctoral researchers at different years and stages of study. Rather than assuming that doctoral independence develops uniformly, the research explores how participants experience changing levels of supervisory support, academic confidence, decision-making responsibility, intellectual ownership and self-directed learning as they progress towards completion.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">A qualitative research design is adopted, using semi-structured interviews with 40 doctoral researchers representing different stages of doctoral study. Participants are invited to reflect upon their learning experiences, relationships with supervisors, changing research responsibilities, confidence in making independent academic decisions, and perceptions of themselves as developing researchers. The analysis examines both common developmental patterns and individual variations in the transition towards doctoral independence.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">The empirical findings are compared with three theoretical perspectives on learning and researcher development. Vygotsky's concept of the More Knowledgeable Other and Zone of Proximal Development provides a framework for examining the more supported stages of doctoral learning, where supervisors may provide significant scaffolding, modelling, feedback and academic guidance. This is expected to be particularly visible among researchers in the earlier stages of doctoral study.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">The experiences of researchers at intermediate stages are compared with andragogical theory, particularly the concept of the adult learner becoming increasingly self-directed and drawing upon accumulated professional, academic and personal experience. The study examines whether doctoral researchers progressively assume greater responsibility for identifying learning requirements, addressing research problems, selecting appropriate methods and managing their own research development.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">Researchers approaching the later stages of their doctorate are considered in relation to heutagogy and self-determined learning. At this stage, the study explores whether doctoral researchers demonstrate greater intellectual autonomy, reflexivity, adaptability and ownership of their research, including the confidence to challenge established thinking, determine their own learning requirements and defend an original contribution to knowledge.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">The study therefore examines a proposed developmental relationship:</p>
                <ul class="text-sm text-muted mt-2 space-y-1 pl-4">
                  <li><strong>Early Doctoral Stage:</strong> Vygotsky → Supported and Scaffolded Learning → Supervisory Dependence</li>
                  <li><strong>Developing Doctoral Stage:</strong> Andragogy → Self-Directed Learning → Increasing Researcher Independence</li>
                  <li><strong>Advanced Doctoral Stage:</strong> Heutagogy → Self-Determined Learning → Intellectual and Researcher Autonomy</li>
                </ul>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">Importantly, the study does not assume that this theoretical progression will be confirmed. Instead, the experiences of the 40 doctoral researchers are used to critically compare, test and refine the proposed relationship between learning theory and the development of doctoral independence. Particular attention is given to researchers whose experiences do not follow a simple linear progression, recognising that doctoral researchers may move between periods of independence and increased supervisory support in response to methodological challenges, research setbacks, significant progression points or changing personal and professional circumstances.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">The study seeks to determine whether the transition from Vygotskian supported learning, through andragogical self-direction, towards heutagogical self-determination provides a useful explanation of the doctoral learning journey. It further examines how the supervisor's role may evolve from More Knowledgeable Other and academic guide, through facilitator and mentor, towards critical friend and scholarly colleague as researcher capability develops.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">The anticipated contribution is an empirically informed Developmental Model of Doctoral Independent Learning, grounded in the lived experiences of doctoral researchers rather than learning theory alone. The model conceptualises doctoral development as a progressive, but potentially non-linear, movement from supervisory dependence through developing independence to intellectual autonomy and self-determined scholarship.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">The findings have potential implications for doctoral programme design, supervisor development, progression monitoring and institutional approaches to researcher support. They suggest that doctoral independence should not simply be expected or assessed at the end of a programme but should be intentionally developed, supported and evaluated throughout the doctoral journey.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-3"><strong>Keywords:</strong> Doctoral Education; Doctoral Researchers; Independent Learning; Doctoral Supervision; Researcher Development; Vygotsky; More Knowledgeable Other; Zone of Proximal Development; Andragogy; Heutagogy; Self-Directed Learning; Self-Determined Learning; Researcher Independence; Intellectual Autonomy.</p>
              </div>

